  <section class="section shell article-content">
    <h2>Why a capability-based microkernel direction?</h2>
    <p class="content-copy">The project's stated direction is to make resource authority explicit, constrain privileged responsibilities and organize system functionality behind defined service boundaries. A capability-based design makes authority grants a central part of the architecture; a microkernel-oriented design aims to keep the privileged core narrow. These are design goals, not claims that particular mechanisms are already implemented or verified.</p>
    <div class="principle-grid">
      <article class="principle-card"><span>01 / KERNEL</span><h3>Constrained privileged core</h3><p>Define which responsibilities must remain privileged and which can be delegated to services. The actual boundary requires public design and implementation evidence.</p></article>
      <article class="principle-card"><span>02 / AUTHORITY</span><h3>Explicit capabilities</h3><p>Specify how authority is represented, granted, transferred, restricted and revoked. Concrete capability semantics remain to be documented and verified.</p></article>
      <article class="principle-card"><span>03 / SERVICES</span><h3>Defined service contracts</h3><p>Document ownership, IPC contracts, lifecycle and failure behavior before claiming isolation or fault containment.</p></article>
      <article class="principle-card"><span>04 / VALIDATION</span><h3>Evidence-led progress</h3><p>Publish source references, test environments, acceptance criteria and limitations for each subsystem when they can be reviewed publicly.</p></article>
    </div>
    <h2>What is publicly established today?</h2>
    <p class="content-copy">The public information currently establishes the project's language and architectural direction, but it does not establish a supported CPU list, a verified boot path, a usable shell, storage behavior, driver coverage or an installable system. Those details should be published only when the project can point to concrete specifications, implementation and repeatable test results.</p>
    <h2>Repository access and contribution</h2>
    <p class="content-copy">The Aurora OS repository is private. The public <a href="https://github.com/Pokejoueur/aurora-website" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">Aurora Website repository</a> contains the source for this website, not the OS implementation. The project's future source-availability policy and any timeline for changing repository visibility have not been published. See <a href="/en/docs/getting-started/">Getting started</a> for the current contribution entry points.</p>
    <h2>Further technical information</h2>
    <p class="content-copy">Read the <a href="/en/engineering/architecture/">architecture overview</a>, the <a href="/en/engineering/security/">security direction</a> and the <a href="/en/development/">development status register</a>. These pages distinguish project direction from implementation evidence.</p>
  </section>
